exports[`nightjar init explains each prompt > prints help before every prompt on Azure DevOps 1`] = `
"  The Tracker is where your Features and Tickets live (issues or work items).
Tracker: github, azure-devops, module [github]: azure-devops
  The first part of your Azure DevOps address, after dev.azure.com/.
Azure DevOps organization, as in dev.azure.com/<organization>: contoso
  The Azure DevOps project that holds the work items.
Project that holds the Features: Fabrikam
  The work item type Nightjar reads as a Feature; its children are the Tickets.
Work item type that plays the Feature, such as Feature, Epic or Product Backlog Item [Feature]: 
  Only \`nightjar create\` needs this: the type it gives the Tickets it makes, such as User Story.
Work item type of a Ticket, for \`nightjar create\` (empty to leave out): User Story
  The Code host is where Nightjar pushes the branch and opens the draft pull request.
Code host: github, azure-devops, module [azure-devops]: azure-devops
  The first part of your Azure DevOps address, after dev.azure.com/.
Azure DevOps organization, as in dev.azure.com/<organization> [contoso]: 
  The Azure DevOps project that holds the git repo.
Project that holds the repo [Fabrikam]: 
  The name of the repo in Azure Repos that the branch is pushed to.
Git repo on Azure Repos, by name: fabrikam-app
nightjar: no check command detected in this repo; try a placeholder such as \`make check\`
  Runs after every Ticket; a non-zero exit means the work failed and the agent retries.
Command that checks the work, run after each Ticket (? for more): make check
  The agent may run only these commands; list what it needs, such as the test runner.
  A pattern ends in \`*\` to take any arguments. Examples: \`make lint *\`, \`npm test\`.
  Commit, push, network and \`gh\` stay denied, whatever is listed.
Commands the agent may run, separated by commas, or none (? for more) [make check]: "
`;

exports[`nightjar init explains each prompt > prints help before every prompt on GitHub, and shows longer help on ? 1`] = `
"  The Tracker is where your Features and Tickets live (issues or work items).
Tracker: github, azure-devops, module [github]: github
  Written as owner/name, such as acme/widgets. Empty takes the repo of your git origin.
GitHub repo whose issues are the Features, owner/name [o/r]: 
  The Code host is where Nightjar pushes the branch and opens the draft pull request.
Code host: github, azure-devops, module [github]: github
  Written as owner/name, such as acme/widgets. Empty takes the repo of your git origin.
GitHub repo the branch is pushed to, owner/name [o/r]: 
nightjar: no check command detected in this repo; try a placeholder such as \`make check\`
  Runs after every Ticket; a non-zero exit means the work failed and the agent retries.
Command that checks the work, run after each Ticket (? for more): ?
  Runs after every Ticket. A non-zero exit means the work failed, and the agent retries.
  Keep it fast and deterministic: typecheck, lint and tests.
  No deploys and no network: it runs many times, and must give the same answer each time.
  Examples: \`npm run check\`, \`make check\`.
Command that checks the work, run after each Ticket (? for more): make check
  The agent may run only these commands; list what it needs, such as the test runner.
  A pattern ends in \`*\` to take any arguments. Examples: \`make lint *\`, \`npm test\`.
  Commit, push, network and \`gh\` stay denied, whatever is listed.
Commands the agent may run, separated by commas, or none (? for more) [make check]: ?
  The agent may run only the commands listed here, separated by commas.
  List everything it needs: the test runner, the linter, and scripts it writes.
  Commit, push, network and \`gh\` are always denied, whatever is listed.
  A pattern ends in \`*\` to take any arguments. Examples: \`make lint *\`, \`npm test\`.
  Type none to allow no command.
Commands the agent may run, separated by commas, or none (? for more) [make check]: "
`;

